--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::field` hands back the four literals a payload folds to and refuses the two ways it can have none, while `Core\Attributes::get` reads the one it cannot — `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant` and `rule:core-classes/reflect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::fields
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::field
// `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant` admits three *named*
// constants beside the literals, and each resolves through the namespace the
// attribute was **written** in. A reflective description carries no such scope —
// it is handed a class the checker never saw — so those three read as *no
// folded value*, which is the bound `Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo::hasValue`
// states one declaration over. This case asserts both sides of it: every
// literal reads back with its own type, and the class constant refuses.
class Limits {
    public const int MAX = 5;
}

#[{count: 3, label: "nightly", ratio: 1.5, live: true, cap: Limits::MAX}]
class Job {}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Job());
foreach ($described->attributes() as Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo $one) {
    // The bare form on the class itself: no written name, no member, no
    // parameter. All three empty is one answer and not three absences.
    echo "name=[", $one->name(), "] target=[", $one->target(), "] parameter=[",
         $one->parameter(), "]\n";

    // Every field keeps its row, the unfoldable one included — the name is as
    // much a fact about the attach site as the value is.
    echo Core\Arr::count($one->fields()), "\n";
    foreach ($one->fields() as string $field) {
        string $shown = "<no folded value>";
        try {
            // Encoded rather than echoed so each value's *type* is asserted
            // beside it: an `int` that read back as text passes neither half.
            $shown = Core\Json::encode($one->field($field));
        } catch (LogicError $unfolded) {
            $shown = "<no folded value>";
        }
        echo "  ", $field, "=", $shown, "\n";
    }

    // The other refusal, and the reason the two are separate throws: a name the
    // payload does not carry is a mistake in the asking, where a name it does
    // carry with nothing to fold is a fact about the declaration. A member that
    // conflated them would report a misspelling as a stated bound.
    try {
        var $missing = $one->field("nosuch");
        echo "read a name it does not carry", "\n";
    } catch (LogicError $wrong) {
        echo "no such field", "\n";
    }
}

// The door that *does* resolve the three named constants, and the reason the
// refusal above costs a program nothing: `Core\Attributes::get` matches by
// shape at compile time, through the scope the payload was written in.
?{cap: int} $folded = Core\Attributes::get<{cap: int}>(Job::constructor(...));
if ($folded != null) { echo "cap=", $folded->cap as string, "\n"; }
--EXPECT--
name=[] target=[] parameter=[]
5
  count=3
  label="nightly"
  ratio=1.5
  live=true
  cap=<no folded value>
no such field
cap=5
